    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d167">

                <pb facs="0280" n="164" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg164"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 711.21/277.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Minister Thomson to the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Extract.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Bogotá</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-06-17T15:00:00-04:56">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 17, 1914, 3 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Apprehending that questions will arise in our Senate regarding the use Colombia
                    may make of the indemnity, the Minister for Foreign Affairs has suggested the
                    desirability of my informing you that <pb facs="0281" n="165" xml:id="pg165"/>
                    while he cannot speak officially for the new administration, which will take
                    office on August 7, it is the desire of those in authority that the indemnity
                    should be invested exclusively in public works, especially railways and the
                    improvement and sanitation of ports, under law enacted by Congress. My own
                    observation leads me to believe that this is the wish of the thinking people as
                    well as the majority of Congress. * * *</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Thomson</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
